        <div id="indoor-safe" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Which Propane Heaters Are Safe Indoors? Only Indoor-Rated Models With ODS
          </h2>

          <p>
            An indoor propane heater is safe to use only if the maker rates it for indoor use and it carries an oxygen depletion sensor, and only with a window cracked and a working carbon monoxide alarm nearby. Every propane flame uses oxygen and produces water vapour and carbon dioxide, plus carbon monoxide if combustion goes wrong. Indoor-rated heaters are built to burn cleanly and shut themselves off when conditions turn unsafe.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Indoor-Rated Portable Heaters</h3>

          <p>
            The Mr. Heater Buddy family is the best-known group: the Portable Buddy, Big Buddy, Buddy FLEX and Little Buddy are all listed as designed and approved for indoor and outdoor use. They run from 1 lb cylinders, or from a 20 lb tank kept outdoors with the right hose and filter, and they need no electricity. Mr. Heater markets the Buddy line with a low-oxygen pilot and a tip-over shutoff; confirm both in the manual of the exact model you buy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Vent-Free Wall Heaters</h3>

          <p>
            Dyna-Glo and ProCom vent-free heaters mount on a wall or stand on feet and run from a gas supply line or an outside tank. They are built for permanent indoor use under their own safety standard, which requires an oxygen depletion sensor pilot. They work without power, and thermostat models hold a steady temperature. Homes with a large outside tank often run other appliances from the same supply, such as a <a href="/best-propane-tankless-water-heater/">propane tankless water heater</a>.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Outdoor-Only Heaters: Never Inside</h3>

          <p>
            Tank-top heaters that screw onto a 20 lb cylinder, patio heaters and most forced-air torpedo heaters are outdoor or construction-site appliances. Never bring them into a living space, and never heat a home with a gas oven, grill or camp stove. Those cause carbon monoxide deaths every winter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What the Safety Systems Do</h3>

          <p>
            An oxygen depletion sensor is a precisely tuned pilot flame. When oxygen in the room falls, the pilot lifts away from its thermocouple and the gas valve closes. A tip-over switch cuts the gas if the heater falls. Neither one measures carbon monoxide directly, which is why a separate CO alarm is not optional.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Size Propane Heater Do I Need? About 20 to 40 BTU Per Square Foot
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most rooms need roughly 20 to 40 BTU per hour for each square foot, depending on insulation, ceiling height and how cold it is outside. The makers' coverage ratings sit at the generous end: the Portable Buddy's 9,000 BTU for 225 square feet works out to 40 BTU per square foot, and the Big Buddy's 18,000 BTU for 450 square feet is the same ratio.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Room Size Rule of Thumb</h3>

          <p>
            Measure the room and multiply length by width. A 12 by 15 foot bedroom is 180 square feet, so a 9,000 BTU Buddy is plenty, while a 3,800 BTU Little Buddy only suits about 95 square feet. In an outage you are aiming to keep one room comfortable, not the whole house, so close doors and heat only the space you are using.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do Not Oversize Indoors</h3>

          <p>
            With a fuel-burning heater, bigger is not safer. Fuel-gas codes treat a space as having enough air for combustion when it has at least 50 cubic feet per 1,000 BTU per hour of appliance input. A 10,000 BTU heater therefore wants at least 500 cubic feet, about a 62 square foot room with an 8 foot ceiling, before any window is opened. Pick the smallest heater that keeps you warm, and use its low setting once the room is up to temperature.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Radiant Coverage Versus Air Temperature</h3>

          <p>
            Radiant heaters warm people and surfaces in front of them, so a room can feel comfortable even while the air is cool. Convection heaters warm the air itself. That affects how you read coverage ratings, which the next sections cover.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Heat One Room, Not the House</h3>

          <p>
            In a power outage the most efficient plan is to choose one room, ideally an interior room with few windows, close its doors, hang a blanket over any drafty doorway and heat only that space. A 9,000 BTU heater that struggles with an open-plan ground floor can keep a closed 200 square foot room comfortable on its low setting, which also stretches your fuel and keeps combustion within what the room's air can handle.
          </p>

