        <div id="cylinders-vs-tank" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. 1 lb Cylinders vs a 20 lb Tank: Runtime, Hose and Filter
          </h2>

          <p>
            A 1 lb propane cylinder holds roughly 21,600 BTU and a 20 lb tank roughly 432,000 BTU. Divide by the heater's BTU per hour and you have the runtime. That arithmetic matches the listings: a 3,800 BTU Little Buddy is listed at up to 5.6 hours per 1 lb cylinder, and the Big Buddy family is listed at up to 216 hours on two 20 lb tanks on its 4,000 BTU low setting.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When 1 lb Cylinders Make Sense</h3>

          <p>
            For short outages and occasional use, 1 lb cylinders are simple: screw one in and light the heater. On a 9,000 BTU high setting, though, one lasts a little over two hours, so a cold night can use four or more. Keep a small stock, stored outdoors or in a detached shed, and never refill disposable cylinders.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Running From a 20 lb Tank</h3>

          <p>
            For a multi-day outage, a 20 lb tank is far cheaper per BTU. The Buddy heaters need a matching hose and a fuel filter, which the listings describe as optional extras. The filter matters: it traps oily residue from the hose that can clog the heater's regulator. The tank itself stays outdoors, with the hose run in through a cracked window or a proper wall fitting.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Never Store Big Tanks Indoors</h3>

          <p>
            Fire codes generally do not allow 20 lb cylinders inside a home, attached garage or basement. Propane is heavier than air, so a leak pools at floor level where a pilot flame can find it. Keep spare tanks outside, upright, shaded and away from doors and windows.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Much Propane to Keep for an Outage</h3>

          <p>
            Work backward from the hours you expect to heat. A Portable Buddy on low burns about 4,000 BTU an hour, so ten hours of heat a day uses about 40,000 BTU, close to two 1 lb cylinders or roughly a tenth of a 20 lb tank. A three-day outage on that pattern needs about six 1 lb cylinders, or a fraction of one 20 lb tank. Double it if you expect to run on high, and keep a spare full tank outdoors through the winter.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ventilation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Do You Need Ventilation for an Indoor Propane Heater? Yes, Always
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every indoor propane heater needs fresh air, even the indoor-rated ones. A burner consumes oxygen and releases water vapour and carbon dioxide into the room. In a modern, tightly sealed house with the furnace off, that air is not being replaced on its own.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Much to Open</h3>

          <p>
            Follow the fresh-air instruction in your heater's manual; Mr. Heater specifies a minimum ventilation opening for indoor use of the Buddy family. In practice that means cracking a window an inch or two in the heated room, or in an adjoining room with the door open. Do not run the heater in a sealed room to save heat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Condensation Is a Sign</h3>

          <p>
            Propane combustion produces a lot of water. If windows fog or walls feel damp after a few hours, the room needs more fresh air. Vent-free wall heaters running for long periods are the biggest moisture source, which is one reason some codes limit them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Headaches Mean Turn It Off</h3>

          <p>
            A headache, dizziness, nausea or unusual drowsiness while a fuel heater runs can be early carbon monoxide poisoning. Turn the heater off, get everyone into fresh air and call for help if symptoms persist. Do not wait for an alarm to confirm it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="co-detectors" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Carbon Monoxide Detectors: Non-Negotiable With Any Propane Heater
          </h2>

          <p>
            An oxygen depletion sensor protects against low oxygen, not carbon monoxide. A dirty burner, a damaged heater or a blocked air supply can produce CO before oxygen drops far enough to trip the pilot. A carbon monoxide alarm is the only thing in the room that actually measures it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where to Put It</h3>

          <p>
            Put a CO alarm in the room with the heater and another outside sleeping areas. Follow the alarm maker's placement advice, keep it away from the heater's immediate heat, and test it before outage season. Our guide to the <a href="/best-carbon-monoxide-detector/">best carbon monoxide detectors</a> compares plug-in, sealed 10-year and smart models.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Battery Backup Is Essential</h3>

          <p>
            A plug-in alarm without a battery is useless in a power outage, which is exactly when you run a propane heater. Use a battery-only alarm or a plug-in model with battery backup, and replace the batteries on schedule.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Smoke Alarms Too</h3>

          <p>
            Any open flame indoors raises fire risk. Make sure smoke alarms on each level work, and keep a fire extinguisher where you can reach it from the heated room.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="radiant-vs-blue-flame" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Radiant vs Blue Flame: Radiant Warms You Fast, Blue Flame Warms the Room
          </h2>

          <p>
            Indoor propane heaters split into two heating styles, and the right one depends on how you use the room.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Radiant and Infrared</h3>

          <p>
            The Buddy heaters and infrared wall heaters like the Dyna-Glo IRSS12LPT-2P heat ceramic or plaque surfaces that radiate warmth onto people and objects. You feel warm within minutes even in a cold room, which is ideal for a family sitting together in one spot during an outage. The trade-off is that areas out of the heater's line of sight stay cool.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Blue Flame Convection</h3>

          <p>
            Blue flame heaters like the Dyna-Glo BFSS10LPT-2P and ProCom ML100TBAHR warm the air, which rises and circulates around the room. Warm-up is slower, but the whole room reaches a more even temperature, and a thermostat can hold it there. They suit rooms you use all day.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which to Choose for an Outage</h3>

          <p>
            For occasional outages and renters, a portable radiant Buddy is the practical answer: no installation, and it goes back in the closet afterwards. For homeowners in areas with long winter outages, a permanently installed blue flame wall heater on an outdoor tank gives steadier heat with less fuss.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="altitude" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Altitude Limits: Why ODS Heaters Shut Off in the Mountains
          </h2>

          <p>
            Oxygen depletion sensors are calibrated for the oxygen content of air at lower elevations. At high altitude the air is thinner, so the pilot can read normal mountain air as low oxygen and shut the heater off.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check the Manual's Limit</h3>

          <p>
            Mr. Heater's manuals for the Buddy family give a maximum altitude of about 7,000 feet, and many vent-free wall heater manuals set a lower limit, often around 4,500 feet. Above the stated limit, the heater may nuisance-trip or may not be approved for use at all. Check the manual of your exact model before you buy if you live in the mountains.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do Not Defeat the Sensor</h3>

          <p>
            If a heater keeps shutting off at altitude, the answer is a different heater, not a modified pilot. Never bypass or tamper with an ODS or tip-over switch. Those devices are what make the heater acceptable indoors at all.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Alternatives at Elevation</h3>

          <p>
            At high altitude, consider a direct-vent propane wall furnace installed by a professional, which draws combustion air from outside, or electric heat backed by a generator or power station.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="propane-vs-kerosene" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Propane vs Kerosene vs Battery Heat in a Power Outage
          </h2>

          <p>
            Propane is the cleanest-burning portable fuel for indoor heat and the easiest to store and handle, which is why it is the first choice for most people planning for winter outages. It is not the only option.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Propane</h3>

          <p>
            Propane stores indefinitely in sealed cylinders, lights instantly and burns cleanly in an indoor-rated heater. Fuel is easy to find at hardware stores and gas stations, and the heaters need no power. Our broader list of the <a href="/best-indoor-heater-for-power-outage/">best indoor heaters for power outages</a> compares propane with every other option.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Kerosene</h3>

          <p>
            Kerosene heaters give more heat per tank and K-1 kerosene is cheap per BTU, but fuel goes stale, smells when lighting and shutting down, and needs careful refilling outdoors. See our guide to the <a href="/best-kerosene-heater/">best kerosene heaters</a> if you are weighing the two.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Battery and Electric</h3>

          <p>
            Battery-powered heaters and power stations cannot run a space heater for long, but they can run an electric blanket or heated pad all night with no combustion at all. For bedrooms, that is often the safest approach. Our <a href="/best-battery-powered-heater/">battery powered heater</a> guide explains what actually works.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safe-setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Safe Setup Checklist: Clearances, Kids, Pets and Sleep
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most propane heater accidents come from how the heater is used, not the heater itself. Run through this list every time you light one indoors.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Clearances</h3>

          <p>
            Keep the heater on a flat, hard, level surface, well clear of curtains, bedding, sofas, paper and clothing. Follow the clearance distances in the manual; radiant heaters need generous space in front of the face. Never dry clothes on or near a heater.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Children and Pets</h3>

          <p>
            Radiant faces and blue flame grilles get hot enough to burn. Keep children and pets away, and do not set a portable heater where it can be knocked over in a busy walkway, even with a tip-over switch.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Turn It Off Before Sleep</h3>

          <p>
            Do not run a portable propane heater while you sleep. Warm the bedroom before bed, turn the heater off, and rely on blankets or an electric blanket on a power station overnight. Permanently installed vent-free heaters follow local code, and many codes bar them from bedrooms.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check for Leaks</h3>

          <p>
            Each time you connect a cylinder or hose, brush soapy water on the connection. Bubbles mean a leak: shut the valve and reconnect. If you smell gas, turn everything off, leave the room and ventilate before trying again. Sealing drafty glass with a <a href="/best-window-insulation-kit/">window insulation kit</a> makes any heater go further, and if you would rather keep the furnace running, see our <a href="/best-generator-for-furnace/">generator for furnace</a> guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Bringing an Outdoor Heater Inside</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Tank-top heaters, patio heaters and most torpedo heaters are not designed for living spaces and have no indoor rating. Using one inside during an outage is a leading cause of winter carbon monoxide poisoning. Only use heaters the listing and manual state are approved for indoor use.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running Without a Carbon Monoxide Alarm</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An oxygen depletion sensor does not detect carbon monoxide. Without a battery-powered or battery-backed CO alarm in the room, there is nothing to warn you if combustion goes wrong. Plug-in alarms with no battery go silent in exactly the power outage you are heating through.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing the Room to Save Heat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Closing every gap to keep warmth in also starves the flame of fresh air and traps water vapour and carbon dioxide. Always leave the ventilation opening the manual specifies, usually a cracked window, even when it feels like wasted heat.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the Biggest Heater for a Small Room</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An 18,000 BTU heater in a small bedroom burns more oxygen and adds more moisture than the room can handle. Size by square footage and air volume, start on high to warm up, then run the lowest setting that keeps you comfortable.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Fuel Filter on a 20 lb Hose</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Running a Buddy heater from a 20 lb tank without the fuel filter Mr. Heater lists as part of the hose setup can let oily residue clog the regulator. That leads to poor flames and failed starts. Fit the filter, keep the tank outdoors and leak-test every connection.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

