        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best indoor propane heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes, the Mr. Heater 9,000 BTU Portable Buddy. It is listed as designed and approved for indoor and outdoor use, has a low-oxygen safety shutoff, heats up to about 225 square feet and needs no electricity. Choose the Big Buddy for larger rooms, or a Dyna-Glo vent-free wall heater if you want permanent thermostatic backup heat.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it safe to use a propane heater indoors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if the heater is rated for indoor use and has an oxygen depletion sensor, and only with fresh air and a working battery-backed carbon monoxide alarm. Keep it clear of anything that burns, turn portable heaters off before sleep, and never use outdoor heaters, grills, camp stoves or a gas oven to heat a living space.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a 1 lb propane cylinder last in a Buddy heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 1 lb cylinder holds roughly 21,600 BTU, so divide that by the heater's output. A Portable Buddy on its 4,000 BTU low setting runs about five hours; on 9,000 BTU high, a little over two. The 3,800 BTU Little Buddy is listed at up to 5.6 hours. For long outages, a 20 lb tank outdoors is cheaper.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to crack a window with an indoor propane heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Every propane flame uses oxygen and releases water vapour and carbon dioxide. Follow the fresh-air opening in your heater's manual, which in practice usually means cracking a window an inch or two in the heated room or a connected room. Foggy windows or a headache are signs the room needs more fresh air.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a 20 lb propane tank indoors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Fire codes generally do not allow 20 lb cylinders inside homes, basements or attached garages, because propane is heavier than air and a leak pools at floor level. Keep the tank outside and run the heater's approved hose in through a cracked window or proper wall fitting, with the fuel filter Buddy heaters need on that hose.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you sleep with a propane heater on?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Do not sleep with a portable propane heater running. Warm the room before bed, turn the heater off and use blankets or a battery-powered electric blanket overnight. Permanently installed vent-free wall heaters follow local building code, and many codes prohibit them in bedrooms. A battery-backed carbon monoxide alarm should be in every sleeping area.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do indoor propane heaters work at high altitude?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often not above the manual's limit. Oxygen depletion sensors are calibrated for lower elevations and can shut the heater off in thin mountain air. Mr. Heater's Buddy manuals list about 7,000 feet as the maximum, and many vent-free wall heaters set a lower limit. Check your exact model, and never tamper with the sensor to keep it running.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

